@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:  <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<https://example.org/odp/refusal> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Ontology Design Pattern"@en ;
    dcterms:title "Ontology Design Pattern"@en ;
    dcterms:description """I'm sorry, but I can't produce an ontology design pattern for this scenario. The scenario description you provided does not contain enough information for me to determine the classes and properties that would be required, and I do not want to invent modelling commitments that could be misleading. If you can share the competency questions, the intended scope of the pattern, and any vocabularies that should be reused, I would be happy to try again. In the meantime, here is a short explanation of what an ontology design pattern usually contains: a small set of classes, the object properties that relate them, domain and range axioms for those properties, and a handful of restrictions capturing the intended cardinality and typing constraints. Without the scenario details, producing such a pattern would amount to guesswork."""@en ;
    rdfs:comment """I'm sorry, but I can't produce an ontology design pattern for this scenario. The scenario description you provided does not contain enough information for me to determine the classes and properties that would be required, and I do not want to invent modelling commitments that could be misleading. If you can share the competency questions, the intended scope of the pattern, and any vocabularies that should be reused, I would be happy to try again. In the meantime, here is a short explanation of what an ontology design pattern usually contains: a small set of classes, the object properties that relate them, domain and range axioms for those properties, and a handful of restrictions capturing the intended cardinality and typing constraints. Without the scenario details, producing such a pattern would amount to guesswork."""@en ;
    skos:note """I'm sorry, but I can't produce an ontology design pattern for this scenario. The scenario description you provided does not contain enough information for me to determine the classes and properties that would be required, and I do not want to invent modelling commitments that could be misleading. If you can share the competency questions, the intended scope of the pattern, and any vocabularies that should be reused, I would be happy to try again. In the meantime, here is a short explanation of what an ontology design pattern usually contains: a small set of classes, the object properties that relate them, domain and range axioms for those properties, and a handful of restrictions capturing the intended cardinality and typing constraints. Without the scenario details, producing such a pattern would amount to guesswork."""@en ;
    owl:versionInfo "1.0.0" .
